    {
      "id": "TDE-02",
      "chapter": "normalization",
      "anchor": "normalization-torsion-degree",
      "title": "A unit coefficient of multiplication by p by degree p²",
      "statement": "For every such good model the integral series [p] has a unit coefficient in some degree i≤p². Each torsion factor first attains its largest coefficient norm in degree zero or two, the degree shift in e² adds two, and taking the square doubles the first residue degree. Combined separately with inert-CM descent this places the first unit in degree exactly p².",
      "hypotheses": "p≥5 is prime. For the last implication one also assumes the inert-CM endomorphism comparison which makes the reduction of e a series in T^{p²}.",
      "definitions": [
        "first nonzero degree after reduction",
        "lowest degree attaining a coefficient maximum",
        "elliptic multiplication series",
        "formal logarithm"
      ],
